WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.410
Every evening, this lamp

2
00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:03.850
switches on by itself.

3
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.980
Today a pupil asked me, “Sir,

4
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:08.320
who switched it on?”

5
00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.200
Nobody touched it.

6
00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.930
So who — or what — noticed

7
00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:14.870
it was getting dark?

8
00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.670
Today: what a sensor is — and how devices

9
00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.050
turn the world around us into numbers.

10
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.740
Your eyes, ears and skin

11
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.800
notice the world around you.

12
00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:29.970
Devices can notice things too.

13
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.820
Many solar lamps have a light sensor.

14
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.170
When it notices the daylight fading, it

15
00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.790
sends a signal, and the lamp switches on.

16
00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.440
Sensors can notice heat, light,

17
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.080
moisture, movement and sound.

18
00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.580
Each one turns what it notices into a

19
00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:52.000
number, or a signal — a message carried

20
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.720
by a change, like a small electric

21
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.680
current that rises and falls.

22
00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.780
So: a sensor notices something in the

23
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.920
world and turns it into a number or

24
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:04.260
signal.

25
00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:06.730
The number it gives at one

26
00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:08.550
moment is called a reading.

27
00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.110
Your hand is a natural sensor.

28
00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:13.970
Touch a pupil’s forehead

29
00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.870
and it says “warm”.

30
00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.540
But “warm” is hard to

31
00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:19.920
write down or compare.

32
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.880
A thermometer senses the same heat

33
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.520
and gives a number — 37.8 degrees

34
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.440
— that anyone can read.

35
00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:30.810
Quick check from my classroom!

36
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.380
I have a loudspeaker, a thermometer and

37
00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:36.980
a notebook of weather readings.

38
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:39.180
Which one is the sensor?

39
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:41.320
The thermometer.

40
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.660
It senses heat and turns it into a number.

41
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.420
The notebook stores

42
00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.480
readings, but senses nothing.

43
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.040
The loudspeaker makes sound

44
00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.420
instead of sensing it.

45
00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:55.330
Sensors are everywhere.

46
00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.980
A thermometer on the classroom wall.

47
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.520
A probe in the school garden that

48
00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.140
reads how wet the soil is.

49
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.100
And in a phone: a microphone for sound, a

50
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.280
camera for light, and a movement sensor

51
00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.100
that notices when you turn it.

52
00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:12.840
A sensor only measures.

53
00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.560
A display shows the reading — a screen,

54
00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.420
a dial, a thermometer’s scale.

55
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.790
An actuator acts — a pump that

56
00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.930
waters, a motor that turns a

57
00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.850
fan, a buzzer that sounds.

58
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.220
In between, a person or a

59
00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:31.580
small computer decides.

60
00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:33.810
Your turn.

61
00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.610
In an automatic watering system,

62
00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.330
a probe finds the soil is dry,

63
00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.230
and a pump switches on.

64
00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.620
Which part is the actuator?

65
00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.560
The pump — it acts.

66
00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.840
The probe is the sensor.

67
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.100
So remember: sensors notice the

68
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.000
world and turn it into numbers.

69
00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.020
Displays show them.

70
00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.010
Actuators act.

71
00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:01.940
Try it in class tomorrow: three bowls of

72
00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:05.020
water — cold, room temperature and warm.

73
00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:08.710
Pupils put one hand in the cold, one in

74
00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:10.930
the warm, then both in the middle.

75
00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:12.870
Their hands disagree!

76
00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:15.150
That’s why we use sensors

77
00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:16.150
that give numbers.
